          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Gates of Hell Project</strong><br>\nThe Gates of Hell was a monumental project commissioned in 1880 that was intended to be a portal for a new decorative arts museum in Paris. The museum was never built, but Rodin worked on the project for over 20 years. It became a laboratory of sorts for his other works, including many sculptures that became famous in their own right, like \"The Thinker\" and \"The Kiss.\"</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Burghers of Calais</strong><br>\nIn 1889, Rodin was commissioned to create \"The Burghers of Calais,\" a sculpture commemorating the six citizens who offered their lives to save their city during the Hundred Years' War. Rodin's innovative design placed the figures at ground level, inviting viewers to engage directly with the emotions and heroism of the subjects. This work reflected Rodin's novel approach to monument and narrative.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Thinker</strong><br>\nOriginally part of \"The Gates of Hell,\" \"The Thinker\" is one of Rodin's best-known works, representing a man in deep contemplation. Over time, the sculpture became a symbol of philosophy and intellectualism. Cast in bronze and positioned as if in mid-thought, \"The Thinker\" reflects Rodin's fascination with the human experience and physical expression.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
