          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Balzac Statue Controversy</strong><br>\nRodin faced another controversy with his statue of French novelist Honoré de Balzac. Commissioned by the Société des Gens de Lettres, Rodin spent nearly a decade studying and creating the sculpture. However, when unveiled in 1898, its abstract nature shocked the literary society, which rejected the work. Despite this, the Balzac statue is now celebrated for its boldness and innovation.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Rodin's Approach to Sculpture</strong><br>\nRodin's approach to sculpture was both traditional and innovative. He used techniques like working in clay before casting in bronze, and he focused on the play of light and shadow to emphasize the texture and form of his subjects. His style diverged from the prevailing decorative approach of his time, focusing instead on conveying the inner psychological life of his subjects through physical form.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Influence</strong><br>\nAuguste Rodin passed away in 1917, leaving behind a legacy as the progenitor of modern sculpture. His works influenced countless artists and his approach reshaped the boundaries of the art form. Museums around the world display Rodin's pieces, and the Musée Rodin in Paris, housed in the hotel where he once worked and lived, continues to preserve and exhibit his vast collection of sculptures and drawings.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
